; INI's render: a tree's events written as one INI document, in the
; dialect this repository's reader builds its values from
; (ts/doc/reference.md). This is a library of definitions, each named
; `ini-...`, which a host links with its own program (alchemy's
; `compile_sources`); its entry point is `ini-render`, and nothing else
; here is the host's to call.
;
; INI is the format whose writer depends most on its reader, so the
; reader's facts come first, each measured on this checkout's reader in
; all three runtimes:
;
; - A value wrapped in double quotes is read as the JSON string inside
;   them, every JSON escape decoded (`\n`, `\t`, `\"`, `\\`, `\uXXXX`, a
;   surrogate pair included), and the quotes count only when they wrap
;   the whole value. So every string value is written double-quoted
;   through `quoted`, as YAML's render writes its strings, and a string
;   holding `=`, `;`, `#`, a quote or a line break reads back as it is.
; - A key wrapped in double quotes is read the same way, so every key is
;   written double-quoted too, and reads back whatever it holds. The one
;   exception is the reader's array mark: a key that ends in `[]` once
;   decoded names an array under the name before the brackets, when
;   that name is not empty.
; - An unquoted value is a string, whatever it looks like, except the
;   whole-value keywords `true`, `false` and `null`, which are read as
;   themselves. So a boolean is written by its name and null as `null`,
;   and both read back; a number is written by its lexeme, or as
;   `Infinity`, `-Infinity` or `NaN`, and reads back as a string, since
;   INI has no numbers. An unquoted value is not cut at a `;`, `#`, `=`
;   or quote inside it while inline comments are off, the default, and
;   one that begins with `[` is a value, not a header; but its `\\` is
;   read as one backslash, and one that begins `[]` and then a comma is
;   refused, so a JSON text written unquoted would not always read back.
; - `key =` with nothing after it reads as the empty string, as `key =
;   ""` does; the render writes the quoted form.
; - A header `[a.b]` opens the section `b` inside `a`, making `a` when
;   it is missing, and a key under the LAST header read lands in that
;   section. A header read again reopens its section and keeps what it
;   held (`section.duplicate: merge`, the default). The root header `[]`
;   returns to the root, under every `section.duplicate`.
; - A header's segments are trimmed, and `\.`, `\]` and `\\` in one are
;   decoded, so a dot, a closing bracket and a backslash in a section
;   name are written so escaped. A section name cannot be quoted. A
;   header is refused when a segment begins with a string in quotes of
;   either kind, `#`, `;`, a comma, `=` or `[`, or is `true`, `false` or
;   `null`, when it holds a line break, and past 127 segments
;   (`DEPTH_LIMIT`); a blank segment names nothing.
; - `key[] = item`, one line per item, builds an array of scalars under
;   `key`; there is no form for an empty array, for an item that is an
;   array or an object, or for an array under the empty key.
;
; The document: the root object's scalar and array members first, one
; `"key" = value` or `"key[]" = item` line each; then each member whose
; value is an object as a section, its header `[path]` on its own line
; and its members under it the same way, a nested object's header being
; `[path.name]`. A header is written when its object opens, so an empty
; section is a header alone. A scalar or array member that arrives after
; a section at the same level comes after that level's own header
; written again, which the reader reads as a return to that section; at
; the root that header is `[]`.
;
; What INI has no place for is written as JSON text: an array or an
; object that is an item of an array, an empty array, an array under the
; empty key, an object whose header would pass 127 segments, and an
; object under a key the render writes no header for. The render writes
; a header for a name only when the name is not blank; holds no control
; character and none of the characters a segment cannot begin with (`"`,
; `'`, `#`, `;`, `,`, `=`, `[`), wherever they stand, since a definition
; cannot ask which character comes first; and is neither `true`, `false`
; nor `null`, nor spelt with blanks and only their letters, which it
; could be once trimmed. The container's compact JSON text is the value
; of its key's line, or of its item's `"key[]" =` line, double-quoted,
; since unquoted it would not always read back (the facts above), so it
; reads back as that text, a string. It is written as its events arrive,
; never held: a key or a string in its JSON form through `quoted`,
; escaped once more for the value that holds the text (each backslash
; doubled, then each quote escaped), a number as a pair's number is, by
; its lexeme or its word, and null, true and false by name.
;
; INI's document is an object: a root of another kind fails with
; TARGET_VALUE_UNREPRESENTABLE, saying what it is. A host that composes
; the render puts any other root under a key of an object first, as the
; manifest's `root` (`object`) asks, so only a caller that runs the
; render directly meets that refusal.
;
; The events are a tree's: an object holds each key once, which the
; host holds a stream to. Events no tree has fail with
; PROTOCOL_ORDER_ERROR, saying so.
;
; The state is a vector of three: the stack of open containers, one
; marker each, so it grows with a document's nesting and never with its
; width; the trail of the open sections' names, which the next header is
; made from; and the key whose value is due, or `:none`, which while an
; array is open is the array's key. The markers: `:scalars` for an
; object whose own header is the last one written, `[:sections last]`
; for one under which the section `last` was written last, `:array-open`
; for an array with no item written yet and `:array` for one with an
; item; inside a JSON text, `:json-object-open` for an object with
; nothing written yet, `:json-object` for one after a member,
; `:json-member` for one whose key is written and waits for its value,
; `:json-array-open` for an array with nothing written yet and
; `:json-array` for one after an item. Once the root object is closed
; the stack is `[:root-done]`, and nothing may follow it.

; The top marker, or :none before the root object.
def ini-top [stack]
  match (count stack)
    case 0 :none
    case _ (top stack)

def ini-key-due "the events hold a value where a key is due, which a tree's never do"
def ini-value-due "the events hold a key where a value is due, which a tree's never do"
def ini-key-in-array "the events hold a key inside an array, which a tree's never do"
def ini-end-in-array "the events end an object while an array is open, which a tree's never do"
def ini-end-after-key "the events end an object after a key and before its value, which a tree's never do"
def ini-end-unopened "the events end an array that is not open, which a tree's never do"

; The deepest header the reader reads, in segments: its DEPTH_LIMIT.
def ini-depth-limit 127

; The characters the reader trims from the ends of a header's segment,
; as code point ranges.
def ini-blank-chars
  [[9 13] [32 32] [127 127] [133 133] [160 160] [5760 5760] [8192 8202] [8232 8233] [8239 8239] [8287 8287] [12288 12288] [65279 65279]]

; The letters of true, false and null: a, e, f, l, n, r, s, t and u.
def ini-keyword-chars
  [[97 97] [101 102] [108 108] [110 110] [114 117]]

; Those letters and the blanks.
def ini-keyword-blank-chars
  [[9 13] [32 32] [97 97] [101 102] [108 108] [110 110] [114 117] [127 127] [133 133] [160 160] [5760 5760] [8192 8202] [8232 8233] [8239 8239] [8287 8287] [12288 12288] [65279 65279]]

; The characters a section name may hold, wherever they stand: all but
; the control characters and the seven a segment cannot begin with,
; `"`, `#`, `'`, `,`, `;`, `=` and `[`.
def ini-name-chars
  [[32 33] [36 38] [40 43] [45 58] [60 60] [62 90] [92 126] [160 1114111]]

; Whether the render writes a header for `name`, to be read back as
; written but for blanks at its ends: it is not blank, it is neither
; true, false nor null nor spelt with blanks and only their letters, and
; it holds no control character and none of the characters a segment
; cannot begin with.
def ini-header-name? [name]
  match (chars-within ini-blank-chars name)
    case true false
    case false
      match (chars-within ini-keyword-blank-chars name)
        case false (chars-within ini-name-chars name)
        case true
          match (chars-within ini-keyword-chars name)
            case false false
            case true
              match name
                case "true" false
                case "false" false
                case "null" false
                case _ true

; Whether an object under `name`, below the sections of `trail`, is
; written as a section: the render writes a header for the name, and the
; header nests no deeper than the reader reads.
def ini-sectionable? [trail name]
  match (compare (count trail) ini-depth-limit)
    case :less (ini-header-name? name)
    case _ false

; A section name as a header segment: a backslash, a dot and a closing
; bracket escaped, the backslash first so the escapes it makes are not
; escaped again.
def ini-segment [name]
  replace-text "]" "\\]"
    replace-text "." "\\."
      replace-text "\\" "\\\\" name

; The header of the section at `trail`: the names dotted, in brackets.
; The root's, with no names, is `[]`.
def ini-header [trail]
  concat "[" (join "." (map ini-segment trail)) "]\n"

; An array item's key, `"name[]"`: the name and the reader's array mark,
; quoted.
def ini-array-key [name]
  quoted (string-join "" [name "[]"])

def ini-number [n]
  match (number-class n)
    case :finite (scalar-text csv-options n)
    case :infinity "Infinity"
    case :negative-infinity "-Infinity"
    case :nan "NaN"

def ini-scalar [value]
  match (kind value)
    case :null "null"
    case :boolean
      match value
        case true "true"
        case false "false"
    case :number (ini-number value)
    case _ (quoted value)

; A root that is not an object: INI's document is one.
def ini-root-scalar [value]
  match (kind value)
    case :null (fail :unrepresentable "INI's document is an object: the document's root is null, which INI cannot carry")
    case :boolean (fail :unrepresentable "INI's document is an object: the document's root is a boolean, which INI cannot carry")
    case :number (fail :unrepresentable "INI's document is an object: the document's root is a number, which INI cannot carry")
    case _ (fail :unrepresentable "INI's document is an object: the document's root is a string, which INI cannot carry")

; What a scalar member begins with, to be read in its object: nothing
; while the object's own header is the last one written, and that
; header again once a section was written after it, the root's being
; `[]`.
def ini-member-lead [stack trail]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :scalars (text "")
    case [:sections _] (ini-header trail)

; The stack once a scalar member has begun: its object's own header is
; the last one written.
def ini-member-stack [stack]
  push :scalars (pop stack)

; An object opens: the root; an item of an array, or the value of a key
; the render writes no header for or of a section nested too deep, as
; JSON text; or a section under the key that is due.
def ini-open [stack trail pending]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :none (transition [[:scalars] trail :none] [])
    case :array-open (ini-json-item stack trail pending "{" :json-object-open)
    case :array (ini-json-item stack trail pending "{" :json-object-open)
    case _
      match pending
        case :none (fail :protocol-order ini-key-due)
        case _
          if (ini-sectionable? trail pending)
            ini-section stack trail pending
            ini-json-pair stack trail pending "{" :json-object-open

; A member whose value is an object is a section: its header is written
; now, so an empty section is a header alone, and its parent's own header
; is no longer the last one written.
def ini-section [stack trail name]
  let [next (push name trail)]
    transition [(push :scalars (push [:sections name] (pop stack))) next :none] [(ini-header next)]

; An array opens under the key that is due. Its items are `"key[]" =
; item` lines, so for the order's sake it is a scalar member. An array
; that is an item of an array, or the value of the empty key, which the
; array mark cannot name, is written as JSON text.
def ini-open-array [stack trail pending]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :none (fail :unrepresentable "INI's document is an object: the document's root is an array, which INI cannot carry")
    case :array-open (ini-json-item stack trail pending "[" :json-array-open)
    case :array (ini-json-item stack trail pending "[" :json-array-open)
    case _
      match pending
        case :none (fail :protocol-order ini-key-due)
        case "" (ini-json-pair stack trail pending "[" :json-array-open)
        case _ (transition [(push :array-open (ini-member-stack stack)) trail pending] [(ini-member-lead stack trail)])

; A container as the value of the key that is due, as the JSON text in
; that key's double-quoted value. For the order's sake it is a scalar
; member.
def ini-json-pair [stack trail pending opener marker]
  transition [(push marker (ini-member-stack stack)) trail pending] [(ini-member-lead stack trail) (quoted pending) " = \"" opener]

; A container as an item of the open array, as the JSON text in the
; double-quoted value of the item's `"key[]" =` line.
def ini-json-item [stack trail pending opener marker]
  transition [(push marker (push :array (pop stack))) trail pending] [(ini-array-key pending) " = \"" opener]

; A key: held until its value says whether it is a pair, a section, an
; array or a JSON text.
def ini-member [stack trail pending name]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :none (fail :protocol-order "the events hold a key outside any object, which a tree's never do")
    case :array-open (fail :protocol-order ini-key-in-array)
    case :array (fail :protocol-order ini-key-in-array)
    case _
      match pending
        case :none (transition [stack trail name] [])
        case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-value-due)

; A scalar: a pair under the key that is due, or an item of the open
; array.
def ini-value [stack trail pending value]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :none (ini-root-scalar value)
    case :array-open (transition [(push :array (pop stack)) trail pending] [(ini-array-key pending) " = " (ini-scalar value) "\n"])
    case :array (transition [stack trail pending] [(ini-array-key pending) " = " (ini-scalar value) "\n"])
    case _
      match pending
        case :none (fail :protocol-order ini-key-due)
        case _ (transition [(ini-member-stack stack) trail :none] [(ini-member-lead stack trail) (quoted pending) " = " (ini-scalar value) "\n"])

; An object closes: the root, and the document is done, or a section,
; and its name leaves the trail. Nothing is written: the next header
; says where the next key goes.
def ini-close [stack trail pending]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :none (fail :protocol-order "the events end an object that is not open, which a tree's never do")
    case :array-open (fail :protocol-order ini-end-in-array)
    case :array (fail :protocol-order ini-end-in-array)
    case _
      match pending
        case :none
          match (count stack)
            case 1 (transition [[:root-done] trail :none] [])
            case _ (transition [(pop stack) (pop trail) :none] [])
        case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-end-after-key)

; An array closes. One that held no item has no `"key[]" =` line, so it
; is written as its JSON text, `[]`, under its key.
def ini-close-array [stack trail pending]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :array-open (transition [(pop stack) trail :none] [(quoted pending) " = \"[]\"\n"])
    case :array (transition [(pop stack) trail :none] [])
    case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-end-unopened)

def ini-event [stack trail pending event]
  match event
    case object-start (ini-open stack trail pending)
    case array-start (ini-open-array stack trail pending)
    case (key name) (ini-member stack trail pending name)
    case (scalar value) (ini-value stack trail pending value)
    case object-end (ini-close stack trail pending)
    case array-end (ini-close-array stack trail pending)

; A key or a string inside a JSON text: its JSON form, escaped once more
; for the double-quoted value that holds the text, each backslash
; doubled before each quote is escaped.
def ini-json-string [s]
  replace-text "\"" "\\\""
    replace-text "\\" "\\\\" (quoted s)

; A scalar inside a JSON text: as a pair's value is written, but a
; string in its JSON form.
def ini-json-scalar [value]
  match (kind value)
    case :string (ini-json-string value)
    case _ (text (ini-scalar value))

; What comes before a value inside a JSON text: a comma after an item of
; an array; nothing before its first item or after a member's key.
def ini-json-lead [stack]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :json-array ","
    case _ ""

; The stack a value begins in inside a JSON text: anywhere but in an
; object waiting for a key, and an array waiting for its first item has
; it.
def ini-json-begin [stack]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :json-object-open (fail :protocol-order ini-key-due)
    case :json-object (fail :protocol-order ini-key-due)
    case :json-array-open (push :json-array (pop stack))
    case _ stack

; The stack once a value inside a JSON text is complete: an object whose
; member it was waits for its next key.
def ini-json-done [stack]
  match (ini-top stack)
    case :json-member (push :json-object (pop stack))
    case _ stack

; A container of a JSON text closes. When it is the text's own, the
; value's closing quote ends the line, and the item or the member it was
; is complete: an array goes on, and an object's key is no longer due.
def ini-json-close [stack trail pending closer]
  let [rest (pop stack)]
    match (ini-top rest)
      case :array (transition [rest trail pending] [closer "\"\n"])
      case :scalars (transition [rest trail :none] [closer "\"\n"])
      case _ (transition [(ini-json-done rest) trail pending] [closer])

def ini-json-event [stack trail pending event]
  match event
    case object-start (transition [(push :json-object-open (ini-json-begin stack)) trail pending] [(ini-json-lead stack) "{"])
    case array-start (transition [(push :json-array-open (ini-json-begin stack)) trail pending] [(ini-json-lead stack) "["])
    case (key name)
      match (ini-top stack)
        case :json-object-open (transition [(push :json-member (pop stack)) trail pending] [(ini-json-string name) ":"])
        case :json-object (transition [(push :json-member (pop stack)) trail pending] ["," (ini-json-string name) ":"])
        case :json-member (fail :protocol-order ini-value-due)
        case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-key-in-array)
    case (scalar value) (transition [(ini-json-done (ini-json-begin stack)) trail pending] [(ini-json-lead stack) (ini-json-scalar value)])
    case object-end
      match (ini-top stack)
        case :json-object-open (ini-json-close stack trail pending "}")
        case :json-object (ini-json-close stack trail pending "}")
        case :json-member (fail :protocol-order ini-end-after-key)
        case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-end-in-array)
    case array-end
      match (ini-top stack)
        case :json-array-open (ini-json-close stack trail pending "]")
        case :json-array (ini-json-close stack trail pending "]")
        case _ (fail :protocol-order ini-end-unopened)

def ini-step [s event]
  match s
    case [stack trail pending]
      match (ini-top stack)
        case :root-done (fail :protocol-order "the events hold more after the root object, which a tree's never do")
        case :json-object-open (ini-json-event stack trail pending event)
        case :json-object (ini-json-event stack trail pending event)
        case :json-member (ini-json-event stack trail pending event)
        case :json-array-open (ini-json-event stack trail pending event)
        case :json-array (ini-json-event stack trail pending event)
        case _ (ini-event stack trail pending event)

def ini-finish [s]
  match s
    case [stack _ _]
      match (ini-top stack)
        case :root-done []
        case :none (fail :protocol-order "the events hold no value, where a tree's hold one")
        case _ (fail :protocol-order "the events ended inside a container")

; The render: the tree's events in, the document's text out.
def ini-render [input]
  join ""
    scan-emit [[] [] :none] ini-step ini-finish (events input)
